I have had numerous debates with one individual over whether or 
not it would be possible to create a program that could play this
game.  Clearly the answer is yes, since the game is a deterministic
finite state machine with random seeds.  

What's not clear is whether a machine in the category of the typical 
workstation could accomplish the task.  

So far, no one seems to have even attempted it.  I wouldn't even 
want to rehash the argument again, until someone has at least produced
something that can do the basic mechanics of the game.  Let's keep it
simple -- how about a program that starts games, rolls N characters, 
chooses among them based on parameters for optimal stat and/or
equipment, and maybe even explores the first level, and leave you 
to continue the game it chooses to be the best candidate of N starts.

Just that would be impressive.  The people who have tried to tell me
that it wouldn't be so hard to do, have also had a quite limited idea
of just how deep Nethack's rabbit hole goes.  Sure, the overall strategy 
is always the same, and the tactics are based on a precisely defined
model, but there is quite a bit of complexity, a whole lot of
combinations, and often a pretty large number of possible decisions at
any given juncture.

I'm betting that a "million monkeys" approach would get you just as far
as any trivial AI.  I've had pretty good runs with a random walk, or
whatever I should call it. 

Someone else suggested an automatic sokoban solver.  Anybody who was
serious about making an automatic nethack player could start there and
generate a great deal of credibility that the rest can be done.  I do
remember an auto player for rogue, but I remember it playing a pretty
sorry game.  NH is far more complicated than that.

Funny that the parent post hints at NH as a plot generator.  I've had
it referred to as exactly that by someone who learned about the game
from me.  

